# Charcha secrets. Copy to `.dev.vars` for local development (it is gitignored),
# and set the real values with `wrangler secret put <NAME>` on a deployment.
#
# The Deploy to Cloudflare button reads this file and asks the deployer for each
# value as it provisions the Worker — "Worker secrets can be defined in a
# `.dev.vars.example` or `.env.example` file with a dotenv format"
# (https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/deploy-buttons/, checked
# 2026-07-25). So a name added here becomes a field in the one-click deploy form;
# a name left out has to be found and set by hand afterwards.
#
# The text shown beside each field is `cloudflare.bindings.<NAME>.description` in
# package.json, same page. Both halves are asserted rather than remembered:
# `pnpm check:deploy` fails if a secret src/ reads is neither listed here nor
# documented in README.md as a `wrangler secret put`, if a name here has no
# description, if a description survives the field it explained, or if a name here
# is read by nothing.
#
# **Leave every value empty.** The deploy form pre-fills from this file, so a
# committed value becomes the default credential on every deployment that accepts
# it — which for the password below would be a published one. `check:deploy` fails
# on a non-empty value for that reason.
#
# **Only two names are live here, and the six commented-out ones below are the
# design rather than an oversight (#139).** The deploy form **requires every field
# it shows**, and Cloudflare documents no way to mark one optional
# (cloudflare/workers-sdk#14075, open, reproduced against the live form). A field
# that must be filled, whose only safe value is nothing, is a trap: a deployer who
# types a placeholder into `TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY` to get past it breaks their own
# comment form silently and permanently, because every verification then fails
# against a key that matches no widget (#104). So the form asks only for the two
# secrets where a value is needed *and* any value the deployer supplies is safe.
# The rest are commented out — still here for local development, invisible to the
# deploy form, and documented in README.md under "Turning on the optional
# features", which is where a deployer is sent to set them.
#
# A `#` line is a comment in every dotenv parser, Cloudflare's included, and if
# that inference is ever wrong the failure is today's behaviour rather than a
# worse one: the six reappear as form fields. It is an inference and not a
# documented fact, so it is on the checklist the first real deploy settles —
# https://github.com/withsetu/charcha/issues/150.
#
# Never commit `.dev.vars`.

# The moderation dashboard's password (#12). Sign in at /admin with this and
# nothing else — there is no user table, no account to create, and no password
# reset. Changing it here and redeploying is also how you sign every session out,
# because sessions are signed with a key derived from this value rather than
# stored.
#
# It is the single credential that can approve, hide and delete every comment on
# the site. Use a generated one — `openssl rand -base64 24` — not a memorable one:
# it is typed once into a password manager, never by hand.
#
# **At least 15 characters** (#120), which is the minimum NIST states for a
# password used as a single-factor authentication mechanism — and there is no
# second factor here. `openssl rand -base64 24` produces 32, so following the line
# above is the whole of the compliance.
#
# Nothing refuses a shorter one, here or at the login. The deploy form has no
# validation hook to refuse with (#139), and a floor at sign-in would lock every
# deployment already running on a short password out of its own dashboard, with no
# reset and no second factor to recover through. Saying it is the whole of the
# check, and it is said in four places: this file, the deploy form's help text in
# package.json, README.md, and the dashboard's Setup tab — which grows a
# "Dashboard password" section when the value is under 15 characters, and stays
# quiet otherwise. A one-click deployer reads the second and the fourth; this file
# is for whoever has a checkout.
#
# Leave it unset and the dashboard refuses every request, including the login.
# That is deliberate: an unconfigured deployment is not an open one.
CHARCHA_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=

# The HMAC key for `comments.ip_hash`, which is what makes the per-IP half of the
# spam rate limit (#8, layer 4) real. Per deployment, and never derivable from the
# database — it is the only thing between the stored hashes and a map of who
# commented from where. Unset means no hash is stored at all, and the per-IP half
# abstains; the per-thread half still runs.
#
# It stays in the deploy form, unlike the four below, because it is the one where
# a hurried deployer cannot hurt themselves: this is an HMAC key, so *any* string
# produces a working rate limit and only its randomness is at stake. Skipping it
# would ship every one-click deployment with the per-IP limit switched off, which
# is worse than a hastily-chosen key on the surface this project exists to defend.
IP_HASH_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set after the deploy, not during it — see the header. Uncomment for local
# development; README.md has the `wrangler secret put` line for each.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The secret key of your Cloudflare Turnstile widget (#8, layer 3). Unset means
# layer 3 abstains and comments are still accepted. The *sitekey* is the widget's
# other half: it is public, it goes on your own page as `data-turnstile-sitekey`,
# and it is not this value. Setting one without the other is #104.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/get-started/server-side-validation/
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# Email notifications (#14) — all three together, or the feature is simply off.
#
# There is no partial state worth having: a key with no recipient has nowhere to
# send, and Charcha has no account and no owner email anywhere in its schema to
# guess one from. Leave all three unset and no email is ever sent, nothing is
# transmitted to anyone, and comments still arrive in the moderation queue at
# /admin — which is where you would read them anyway.
#
# Before these are worth filling in, you need a domain **verified in Resend**.
# Resend's own `resend.dev` sender can only mail the address on your Resend
# account; sending to anyone else from an unverified domain is answered 403.
# https://resend.com/docs/knowledge-base/403-error-resend-dev-domain
#
# Enabling this sends the commenter's display name and an excerpt of their comment
# to Resend, a third party. It does **not** send their email address or anything
# derived from their IP. If your site has a privacy notice, the first sentence is
# the one that belongs in it.

# A Resend API key. https://resend.com/docs/api-reference/introduction
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# The two addresses are **not here any more** (#207). Where notifications are sent
# from and where they go are your own configuration rather than credentials, so
# they are settings rows edited under **Setup** in your Charcha dashboard — no
# terminal, no redeploy. A sender name lives beside them (#208).
#
# `CHARCHA_NOTIFY_FROM` and `CHARCHA_NOTIFY_TO` are still read on a deployment that
# set them before this changed, and only until the matching field is saved. See
# README, **Turning on the optional features**.

# Third-party spam checking (#11, layer 7) — both together, or the layer is off.
#
# **Read this before you set either.** Charcha's other six spam layers run inside
# your Worker and transmit nothing about your readers to anybody. This one does
# not. Every comment that reaches layer 7 is posted to Akismet, a service run by
# Automattic, carrying the commenter's **IP address in full** (Akismet requires
# it — it is not the hash the rate limit stores), their name, their email address
# if they gave one, the whole text of their comment, their browser's user-agent
# string, the page they were referred from, and the page they commented on. That
# is a disclosure obligation you take on, not one Charcha can take on for you, and
# it covers readers whose comments you later delete — the check happens before the
# comment is stored. docs/spam-providers.md has the paragraph to paste into a
# privacy notice, and the full field list.
#
# What it buys: a held comment, never a refused one. Layer 7 can only put a
# comment in your moderation queue with `provider: akismet` as its reason. It runs
# last, so it sees only what the six free layers could not decide, and it is
# skipped entirely for a comment something else already held — Akismet's paid tier
# is 500 checks a month (https://akismet.com/pricing/, checked 2026-07-29), and an
# anonymous visitor must not be able to spend them.
#
# Leave both unset and nothing is sent to anyone, ever, which is the default on
# every deployment and the right answer for most sites.

# An Akismet API key, from https://akismet.com/account/. Unset means layer 7 is
# off and no comment leaves your Worker.
# https://akismet.com/developers/comment-check/
# AKISMET_API_KEY=

# Your site's home page is **not here any more** (#207). It is the public address
# of your own website rather than a credential, so it is a settings row edited
# under **Setup** in your Charcha dashboard. Akismet requires it (`blog`) and
# matches it against the sites authorised on your key, and it is the base for the
# permalink sent with each check — so saving the key without it leaves layer 7 off,
# and one line in your Worker's log says which half is missing.
#
# `CHARCHA_SITE_URL` is still read on a deployment that set it before this changed,
# and only until the field is saved. See README, **Turning on the optional
# features**.
